1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,240
The last video ended at the machine.

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:07,520
The streaming multiprocessors, the

3
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:09,920
tensor cores, and the four-level ladder

4
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:12,120
of memory that feeds them.

5
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:13,720
This video is about the code that runs

6
00:00:12,120 --> 00:00:16,880
on that machine.

7
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:19,520
That code comes in units called kernels.

8
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:21,840
A kernel is one small program.

9
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:23,960
The CPU hands it to the GPU as a single

10
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:26,440
piece of work, say,

11
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:28,960
multiplying two matrices

12
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:31,280
or normalizing the rows of one.

13
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:33,840
The GPU then runs that one program

14
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:36,000
across thousands of threads at once.

15
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:37,360
Everything the chip ever does happens

16
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,040
this way.

17
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:42,080
When PyTorch multiplies two matrices,

18
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:44,160
what actually runs on the silicon is a

19
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:45,840
kernel.

20
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:49,040
Two kernels can compute the same

21
00:00:45,840 --> 00:00:51,560
attention on the same GPU with identical

22
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:54,280
inputs and identical outputs.

23
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:56,280
One of them can finish up to 7.6 times

24
00:00:54,280 --> 00:00:58,400
faster than the other.

25
00:00:56,280 --> 00:01:00,560
The arithmetic cannot be the difference

26
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:02,320
because both kernels perform exactly the

27
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:03,760
same operations.

28
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:06,720
What separates [music] them is

29
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:08,775
everything the mathematics leaves open.

30
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:08,880
Which threads load which bytes

31
00:01:08,775 --> 00:01:10,840
>> [music]

32
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:13,440
>> from which level of memory, in what

33
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:15,920
order, at what precision, and where each

34
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:18,160
result is written. Every one of those is

35
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:20,680
a choice about moving data.

36
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:22,880
The whole gap lives there in the data

37
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:26,000
movement.

38
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:27,800
We will follow one lineage of kernels,

39
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,400
FlashAttention,

40
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:32,640
through four generations. It computes

41
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,480
attention, the operation at the heart of

42
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:37,280
the transformer.

43
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:39,600
Once context grows long, attention's

44
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:41,440
memory cost grows with it, and it

45
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:44,000
becomes a dominant draw on memory

46
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:45,680
bandwidth in the forward pass.

47
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,040
That is what makes it one of the kernels

48
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:50,080
that matter most for inference.

49
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:52,800
Across all four generations, the

50
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:55,080
function never changes. Every generation

51
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:57,880
computes the same attention, the same

52
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:59,760
scores, the same softmax, the same

53
00:01:57,880 --> 00:02:01,760
weighted sum.

54
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:03,800
What changes is the fraction of the

55
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:05,760
chip's peak arithmetic the kernel

56
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:08,280
actually delivers.

57
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:10,520
It starts low, the tensor cores mostly

58
00:02:08,280 --> 00:02:12,920
waiting on memory, and climbs to roughly

59
00:02:10,520 --> 00:02:14,920
3/4 of peak.

60
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:17,120
A gap that wide with identical

61
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:20,920
mathematics has to come from somewhere

62
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:22,680
physical, from where the bytes sit.

63
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:25,040
Flash attention is the one kernel this

64
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:26,560
video traces in full, four generations

65
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:28,120
of it.

66
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:29,640
After it come the other levers of

67
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:33,160
inference.

68
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:35,000
A model built to keep a smaller cache,

69
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:36,400
a cache backed into memory without

70
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:39,080
waste,

71
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:41,240
decode work split so the chip is not

72
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:44,360
idle on one token,

73
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:47,920
the launch of each kernel paid for once,

74
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:49,480
and every number carried in fewer bits.

75
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:51,440
Two tiers of the memory ladder we

76
00:02:49,480 --> 00:02:53,120
climbed last time decide everything that

77
00:02:51,440 --> 00:02:54,886
follows.

78
00:02:53,120 --> 00:02:55,120
The first is HBM,

79
00:02:54,886 --> 00:02:57,080
>> [music]

80
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:58,920
>> the high bandwidth memory stacked beside

81
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:02,080
the GPU die.

82
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:06,120
It is the large tier, and H100 holds 80

83
00:03:02,080 --> 00:03:07,680
GB of it, and streams them at 3.35 TB

84
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:09,959
per second.

85
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:12,239
For an accelerator this hungry, even

86
00:03:09,959 --> 00:03:14,239
that counts as slow.

87
00:03:12,239 --> 00:03:17,040
The second tier is SRAM.

88
00:03:14,239 --> 00:03:19,320
There is up to 228 KB of it per

89
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:22,840
streaming multiprocessor, and the chip

90
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:25,200
has 132 of those multiprocessors.

91
00:03:22,840 --> 00:03:27,959
SRAM sits right beside the arithmetic

92
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:30,440
units, and it runs about 10 times faster

93
00:03:27,959 --> 00:03:32,640
than HBM.

94
00:03:30,440 --> 00:03:35,000
Compute throughput has outgrown memory

95
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:37,720
bandwidth for generations now.

96
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
So, moving a byte across that gap costs

97
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:41,760
more than doing arithmetic on it once it

98
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,560
arrives.

99
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:46,280
The whole craft of writing a kernel

100
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:49,160
reduces to one habit, keep the bytes out

101
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:51,800
of HBM and next to the math.

102
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:54,640
And which side a kernel lands on, memory

103
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:56,880
or compute, is decided by a single ratio

104
00:03:54,640 --> 00:04:00,120
we already know.

105
00:03:56,880 --> 00:04:02,160
That ratio is arithmetic intensity.

106
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:03,800
The operations a kernel performs per

107
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:05,360
byte it reads.

108
00:04:03,800 --> 00:04:07,080
It lives on a diagram we have built

109
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:09,200
twice, [music] the roofline.

110
00:04:07,080 --> 00:04:11,920
The roofline has a flat ceiling set by

111
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:14,400
the Tensor Cores, a slanted ceiling set

112
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:16,000
by memory bandwidth, and a ridge where

113
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:19,000
the two meet.

114
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,320
On the H100, that ridge sits at roughly

115
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:26,000
295 operations per byte.

116
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:28,880
A large dense matrix multiply does a lot

117
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,640
of arithmetic on every byte it loads, so

118
00:04:28,880 --> 00:04:32,919
it sits well past the ridge,

119
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:34,720
compute-bound, with the Tensor Cores

120
00:04:32,919 --> 00:04:36,520
setting the pace.

121
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:38,919
Attention over long sequences is the

122
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:41,320
opposite. It does very little arithmetic

123
00:04:38,919 --> 00:04:44,040
per byte it touches, so it sits far down

124
00:04:41,320 --> 00:04:47,160
the slanted ceiling, memory-bound.

125
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:51,080
Memory-bound means capped by bandwidth,

126
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:53,400
however fast the math units are.

127
00:04:51,080 --> 00:04:54,760
For a kernel author, the roofline is a

128
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:57,080
lever.

129
00:04:54,760 --> 00:04:58,160
The function fixes how many operations

130
00:04:57,080 --> 00:05:01,520
there are.

131
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:05,080
Nothing fixes how many bytes move.

132
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:07,760
Cut the HBM traffic and intensity rises,

133
00:05:05,080 --> 00:05:10,880
and the same computation slides right

134
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:12,960
out from under the bandwidth ceiling.

135
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:15,080
That is the craft, and its measure is

136
00:05:12,960 --> 00:05:17,400
the fraction of peak arithmetic a kernel

137
00:05:15,080 --> 00:05:18,840
sustains, the number we will track from

138
00:05:17,400 --> 00:05:20,520
here on.

139
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:24,600
The workload that needed this most

140
00:05:20,520 --> 00:05:24,600
urgently was attention itself.

141
00:05:26,919 --> 00:05:30,160
The reason is visible in attention's

142
00:05:28,640 --> 00:05:31,919
definition.

143
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:34,560
From the first video, each token builds

144
00:05:31,919 --> 00:05:37,360
three [music] things: a query, what this

145
00:05:34,560 --> 00:05:40,280
token is looking for, a key, what it

146
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:42,960
offers to other tokens, and a value,

147
00:05:40,280 --> 00:05:45,800
what it actually contributes.

148
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:49,280
Stack those over a sequence of N tokens

149
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:52,080
and you get three matrices, Q, K, and V,

150
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:55,840
each with n rows, one per token, and d

151
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:57,440
columns, where d is typically 64 to 128

152
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:59,600
per head.

153
00:05:57,440 --> 00:06:02,160
The kernel's first step compares every

154
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:05,480
query against every key, written as a

155
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:06,920
matrix product, that is S = Q * K

156
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:09,200
transpose.

157
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:11,600
One multiply does all the comparisons at

158
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:15,160
once, and the result is a score matrix

159
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:17,400
with one entry for every pair of tokens.

160
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:19,720
The second step turns each row of raw

161
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:22,320
scores into weights. The function that

162
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:24,000
does it is called softmax.

163
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:26,680
The raw scores come out of the

164
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:29,160
comparison at any size, positive or

165
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:31,520
negative. What we need instead are

166
00:06:29,160 --> 00:06:33,160
blending weights, positive numbers that

167
00:06:31,520 --> 00:06:36,120
sum to one.

168
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:39,040
Softmax does this in two moves.

169
00:06:36,120 --> 00:06:41,160
First, raise e to each score. Every

170
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:44,280
number becomes positive, and strong

171
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:46,160
scores pull far ahead of weak ones.

172
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:49,360
Then divide each result by the row's

173
00:06:46,160 --> 00:06:50,689
total, so the row sums to one.

174
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:51,080
That is the whole function.

175
00:06:50,689 --> 00:06:54,480
>> [music]

176
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:56,560
>> Exponentiate, then normalize.

177
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:58,760
There is one practical wrinkle.

178
00:06:56,560 --> 00:07:01,080
The exponential of a score overflows

179
00:06:58,760 --> 00:07:03,640
finite precision floating-point well

180
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:05,880
before the score itself looks large.

181
00:07:03,640 --> 00:07:07,960
So, every real implementation first

182
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:09,029
subtracts the row's largest score from

183
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:09,200
every entry.

184
00:07:09,029 --> 00:07:11,240
>> [music]

185
00:07:09,200 --> 00:07:13,480
>> The weights come out identical. The

186
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:16,240
largest exponent becomes zero, and

187
00:07:13,480 --> 00:07:18,640
nothing overflows.

188
00:07:16,240 --> 00:07:21,360
The third step multiplies the weights by

189
00:07:18,640 --> 00:07:22,960
V, blending the values into one output

190
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:25,880
row per token.

191
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:28,919
So, attention is two matrix multiplies

192
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:31,560
with a normalization between them.

193
00:07:28,919 --> 00:07:33,800
One more piece from the first video.

194
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:35,760
Attention runs not once per layer, but

195
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:38,680
as a few dozen independent copies side

196
00:07:35,760 --> 00:07:41,125
by side, called heads. Each head has its

197
00:07:38,680 --> 00:07:41,480
own smaller Q, K, and V.

198
00:07:41,125 --> 00:07:43,640
>> [music]

199
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:46,640
>> Each head is free to track a different

200
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:48,880
kind of relationship between tokens.

201
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:52,400
Everything in this video happens inside

202
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:54,680
a single head, so we follow one.

203
00:07:52,400 --> 00:07:57,720
The trouble is the object in the middle.

204
00:07:54,680 --> 00:08:01,080
S holds one score per pair of tokens, so

205
00:07:57,720 --> 00:08:04,640
it is n by n. Double the sequence length

206
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:06,760
and its size quadruples. At 1,024

207
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:07,960
tokens, it holds about a million

208
00:08:06,760 --> 00:08:10,640
entries.

209
00:08:07,960 --> 00:08:16,200
At 8,192 [music]

210
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:19,200
tokens, 67 million. At 32,768

211
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:21,680
tokens, about a billion.

212
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:24,600
A naive kernel builds every one of those

213
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:26,480
entries and makes three separate passes

214
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:29,280
through HBM.

215
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:31,520
The first pass computes S and writes it

216
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:33,479
out in full because the next kernel

217
00:08:31,520 --> 00:08:36,400
needs it as input.

218
00:08:33,479 --> 00:08:38,719
The second pass reads S back, applies

219
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:40,719
the softmax row by row, and writes the

220
00:08:38,719 --> 00:08:43,760
result out again.

221
00:08:40,719 --> 00:08:47,440
The third pass reads it one more time

222
00:08:43,760 --> 00:08:49,600
and multiplies by V for the output.

223
00:08:47,440 --> 00:08:52,000
The arithmetic here is mostly matrix

224
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:54,000
multiplication, exactly what the tensor

225
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:56,480
cores are built for.

226
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:58,960
The bottleneck is the traffic.

227
00:08:56,480 --> 00:09:02,120
The bytes grow as n squared while the

228
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:04,560
work done per byte stays small. So on

229
00:09:02,120 --> 00:09:06,920
long sequences, attention lands deep in

230
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:09,440
the memory-bound corner of the roofline,

231
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:11,240
the bus saturated and the tensor cores

232
00:09:09,440 --> 00:09:14,400
waiting.

233
00:09:11,240 --> 00:09:17,400
The same cost model points at the cure.

234
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:20,720
S is scratch, produced once, consumed

235
00:09:17,400 --> 00:09:23,240
once, then discarded. Build it on chip

236
00:09:20,720 --> 00:09:25,880
and never write it to HBM and the n

237
00:09:23,240 --> 00:09:29,360
squared traffic vanishes.

238
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:31,760
That cure has a name, flash attention.

239
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:34,640
Flash attention was published in 2022 by

240
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:36,920
Tri Dao and his collaborators.

241
00:09:34,640 --> 00:09:39,040
It begins with a change of accounting

242
00:09:36,920 --> 00:09:40,960
rather than a new algorithm.

243
00:09:39,040 --> 00:09:42,240
The starting point is the fact from a

244
00:09:40,960 --> 00:09:44,600
moment ago.

245
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:46,760
Attention is memory bound.

246
00:09:44,600 --> 00:09:49,600
It's wall clock time is set by the bytes

247
00:09:46,760 --> 00:09:52,200
moved, not the operations performed. So,

248
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:53,920
the paper changes what it counts.

249
00:09:52,200 --> 00:09:56,920
The cost model becomes the reads and

250
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:59,480
writes that cross between HBM and SRAM,

251
00:09:56,920 --> 00:10:03,320
the two tiers we just compared.

252
00:09:59,480 --> 00:10:05,520
The authors call this IO awareness.

253
00:10:03,320 --> 00:10:08,200
Under that accounting, the three-pass

254
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:09,280
implementation is expensive for one

255
00:10:08,200 --> 00:10:12,480
reason.

256
00:10:09,280 --> 00:10:15,600
It parks two full n by n matrices in

257
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:16,720
HBM, the raw scores and the softmax

258
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:18,920
weights.

259
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:20,360
Then it pays the bus cost to read each

260
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:22,560
one back.

261
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:24,960
The cheap place to work is SRAM with

262
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:26,920
about 10 times the bandwidth, but SRAM

263
00:10:24,960 --> 00:10:28,760
holds only a couple hundred kilobytes

264
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:31,920
per multiprocessor.

265
00:10:28,760 --> 00:10:34,560
The n by n score matrix will never fit

266
00:10:31,920 --> 00:10:36,160
their whole at any realistic sequence

267
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:38,400
length.

268
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:40,400
What rescues the plan is one property of

269
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:43,839
matrix multiplication.

270
00:10:40,400 --> 00:10:47,160
It decomposes cleanly into blocks.

271
00:10:43,839 --> 00:10:50,040
So, Flash Attention cuts Q, K, and V

272
00:10:47,160 --> 00:10:52,120
into block size to fit in SRAM.

273
00:10:50,040 --> 00:10:54,520
This is called tiling.

274
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:56,400
The kernel loads one block of K and one

275
00:10:54,520 --> 00:10:58,839
block of V on chip.

276
00:10:56,400 --> 00:11:01,280
For each block of Q, it computes that

277
00:10:58,839 --> 00:11:03,800
tile of the attention right there, folds

278
00:11:01,280 --> 00:11:07,360
the partial result into the output, then

279
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:10,680
lets the next blocks of K and V arrive.

280
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:13,400
Each tile of S lives briefly in SRAM,

281
00:11:10,680 --> 00:11:16,120
gets used, and gets overwritten. The

282
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:20,240
full n by n matrix is built tile by tile

283
00:11:16,120 --> 00:11:22,520
on chip and never reaches HBM at all.

284
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:25,400
This is still exact attention.

285
00:11:22,520 --> 00:11:27,160
It does not skip token pairs or accept a

286
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:29,000
noisy answer.

287
00:11:27,160 --> 00:11:31,640
The output matches the three-pass

288
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:34,120
version identical up to the reordering

289
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:36,240
of floating point sums. The function did

290
00:11:34,120 --> 00:11:37,720
not change. The route the bytes take

291
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:39,760
did.

292
00:11:37,720 --> 00:11:41,320
One obstacle remains, and it is the

293
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:43,478
central one.

294
00:11:41,320 --> 00:11:43,520
Softmax still wants the whole row,

295
00:11:43,478 --> 00:11:45,200
>> [music]

296
00:11:43,520 --> 00:11:46,960
>> making it run without ever seeing the

297
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:48,800
whole row is the other half of the

298
00:11:46,960 --> 00:11:51,360
paper.

299
00:11:48,800 --> 00:11:53,040
Softmax needs two row wide numbers, the

300
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:54,800
rows maximum and its sum of

301
00:11:53,040 --> 00:11:56,720
exponentials.

302
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:59,640
Those are exactly what tiling cannot

303
00:11:56,720 --> 00:12:00,760
hand it. A tile only ever sees a slice

304
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:02,480
of a row.

305
00:12:00,760 --> 00:12:05,000
And every weight has to divide by the

306
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:07,720
rows sum, while every exponential has to

307
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:09,840
subtract the rows maximum.

308
00:12:07,720 --> 00:12:11,680
So, as Softmax is normally written,

309
00:12:09,840 --> 00:12:14,400
nothing in the row can be finished until

310
00:12:11,680 --> 00:12:16,440
the whole row exists.

311
00:12:14,400 --> 00:12:17,400
The fix predates flash attention by 4

312
00:12:16,440 --> 00:12:20,120
years.

313
00:12:17,400 --> 00:12:23,240
It is called online Softmax published

314
00:12:20,120 --> 00:12:25,080
out of Nvidia in 2018 by Milakov and

315
00:12:23,240 --> 00:12:27,120
Gimel'shein.

316
00:12:25,080 --> 00:12:29,720
It computes the same result in a single

317
00:12:27,120 --> 00:12:32,320
streaming pass by carrying both row wide

318
00:12:29,720 --> 00:12:34,480
numbers as running values.

319
00:12:32,320 --> 00:12:36,560
One is a running maximum, the largest

320
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:38,498
score seen so far.

321
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:38,520
The other is a running denominator,

322
00:12:38,498 --> 00:12:41,160
>> [music]

323
00:12:38,520 --> 00:12:42,720
>> the sum of exponentials so far.

324
00:12:41,160 --> 00:12:45,000
The subtlety is what happens when the

325
00:12:42,720 --> 00:12:47,600
maximum changes partway through.

326
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:49,800
Watch it on one row.

327
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:52,200
The first tile of scores arrives, and

328
00:12:49,800 --> 00:12:54,480
the largest score in it is three. So, we

329
00:12:52,200 --> 00:12:56,360
compute each exponential as e to the

330
00:12:54,480 --> 00:13:00,120
score minus three, and the running

331
00:12:56,360 --> 00:13:01,880
denominator accumulates to 1.59.

332
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:04,480
Then the next tile arrives carrying a

333
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:05,920
five, and the running maximum updates to

334
00:13:04,480 --> 00:13:07,560
five.

335
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:09,800
But every exponential already folded

336
00:13:07,560 --> 00:13:11,880
into that 1.59 subtracted the old

337
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:14,520
maximum, three.

338
00:13:11,880 --> 00:13:17,080
The true maximum is now five.

339
00:13:14,520 --> 00:13:19,080
So, each of those terms is too large by

340
00:13:17,080 --> 00:13:21,120
exactly [music] the same factor, e to

341
00:13:19,080 --> 00:13:22,800
the five minus three.

342
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:24,680
They all subtracted the same wrong

343
00:13:22,800 --> 00:13:26,280
number, so they are all wrong by the

344
00:13:24,680 --> 00:13:27,960
same amount.

345
00:13:26,280 --> 00:13:29,760
That shared error what saves the

346
00:13:27,960 --> 00:13:31,600
algorithm.

347
00:13:29,760 --> 00:13:35,240
One multiplication repairs the whole

348
00:13:31,600 --> 00:13:38,960
denominator at once. We scale the 1.59

349
00:13:35,240 --> 00:13:41,440
by E to the 3 - 5. That factor is about

350
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:44,080
0.135.

351
00:13:41,440 --> 00:13:45,080
Every accumulated term lands on the new

352
00:13:44,080 --> 00:13:47,640
scale,

353
00:13:45,080 --> 00:13:50,400
ready for the new tiles exponentials to

354
00:13:47,640 --> 00:13:51,720
add on cleanly.

355
00:13:50,400 --> 00:13:54,000
Flash attention pushes the same

356
00:13:51,720 --> 00:13:55,800
correction one step further.

357
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:58,360
The running output is built from those

358
00:13:55,800 --> 00:14:00,720
same exponentials, so it drifts off

359
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:03,240
scale in exactly the same way.

360
00:14:00,720 --> 00:14:05,320
The identical factor repairs it.

361
00:14:03,240 --> 00:14:07,360
Whenever the denominator rescales, the

362
00:14:05,320 --> 00:14:09,520
output rescales with it, and the two

363
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:11,800
stay in step.

364
00:14:09,520 --> 00:14:13,520
By the last tile, the running maximum is

365
00:14:11,800 --> 00:14:16,640
the true row maximum, [music] and the

366
00:14:13,520 --> 00:14:18,640
running denominator is the true row sum.

367
00:14:16,640 --> 00:14:21,040
The output matches a full row softmax

368
00:14:18,640 --> 00:14:23,800
exactly because the correction was exact

369
00:14:21,040 --> 00:14:26,440
at every step along the way.

370
00:14:23,800 --> 00:14:28,720
This is what makes tiling legitimate.

371
00:14:26,440 --> 00:14:32,839
Attention never needed the whole row at

372
00:14:28,720 --> 00:14:35,000
once, only two running numbers per row.

373
00:14:32,839 --> 00:14:37,440
That settles the forward pass, the only

374
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:39,800
pass inference ever runs. What comes

375
00:14:37,440 --> 00:14:41,920
next belongs to training.

376
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:44,260
Since inference never runs the backward

377
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:44,560
pass, treat it as a brief detour,

378
00:14:44,260 --> 00:14:47,280
>> [music]

379
00:14:44,560 --> 00:14:50,520
>> but one worth taking because it finishes

380
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:53,320
how flash attention handles its memory.

381
00:14:50,520 --> 00:14:55,839
Training runs every computation twice.

382
00:14:53,320 --> 00:14:57,920
The forward pass makes a prediction. The

383
00:14:55,839 --> 00:15:00,040
backward pass then runs through the same

384
00:14:57,920 --> 00:15:01,760
steps to work out how each weight should

385
00:15:00,040 --> 00:15:03,880
change.

386
00:15:01,760 --> 00:15:06,839
And that backward sweep needs the

387
00:15:03,880 --> 00:15:09,680
attention matrix a second time.

388
00:15:06,839 --> 00:15:11,920
Flash attention never stored it.

389
00:15:09,680 --> 00:15:13,800
Instead of storing the matrix, flash

390
00:15:11,920 --> 00:15:14,560
attention kept just enough to rebuild

391
00:15:13,800 --> 00:15:16,160
it.

392
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:19,280
That was the two running numbers per

393
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:21,080
row, the maximum and the denominator,

394
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:22,640
which online softmax was already

395
00:15:21,080 --> 00:15:24,720
carrying.

396
00:15:22,640 --> 00:15:28,200
During the backward pass, the kernel

397
00:15:24,720 --> 00:15:30,240
reloads Q, K, and V tile by tile.

398
00:15:28,200 --> 00:15:33,040
It recomputes each tile of the attention

399
00:15:30,240 --> 00:15:34,920
matrix on chip at the exact moment that

400
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:37,080
tile is needed. The extra

401
00:15:34,920 --> 00:15:40,080
multiplications are cheap since they run

402
00:15:37,080 --> 00:15:42,160
on data already sitting in SRAM. The HBM

403
00:15:40,080 --> 00:15:44,000
traffic they avoid is exactly the

404
00:15:42,160 --> 00:15:46,520
expensive kind.

405
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,520
So, attention's memory footprint drops

406
00:15:46,520 --> 00:15:50,160
from quadratic in the sequence length to

407
00:15:48,520 --> 00:15:52,840
linear.

408
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:55,320
That is the principle kernel runs on.

409
00:15:52,840 --> 00:15:57,080
Never spend HBM traffic on anything you

410
00:15:55,320 --> 00:15:58,680
can rebuild from what is already on

411
00:15:57,080 --> 00:16:00,560
chip.

412
00:15:58,680 --> 00:16:02,600
Tiling, online SoftMax, and

413
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:03,920
recomputation together are the whole

414
00:16:02,600 --> 00:16:07,720
kernel.

415
00:16:03,920 --> 00:16:10,440
On GPT-2, it runs attention up to 7.6

416
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:12,200
times faster than the standard PyTorch

417
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:15,080
implementation.

418
00:16:12,200 --> 00:16:17,360
That measured 7.6 is the figure this

419
00:16:15,080 --> 00:16:19,600
video opened with.

420
00:16:17,360 --> 00:16:22,200
Judged on the measure we set up though,

421
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:25,240
this kernel still sustains only 25 to

422
00:16:22,200 --> 00:16:27,120
40% of peak arithmetic.

423
00:16:25,240 --> 00:16:29,760
The memory wall is handled, and the

424
00:16:27,120 --> 00:16:32,600
bytes now take the short route.

425
00:16:29,760 --> 00:16:34,960
Yet, the Tensor Cores still sit idle

426
00:16:32,600 --> 00:16:37,680
most of the time for reasons that have

427
00:16:34,960 --> 00:16:39,120
nothing to do with HBM.

428
00:16:37,680 --> 00:16:42,160
The next three generations of this

429
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:45,040
kernel exist because of that number.

430
00:16:42,160 --> 00:16:47,200
Tri Dao's follow-up, Flash Attention 2,

431
00:16:45,040 --> 00:16:49,320
came out in 2023.

432
00:16:47,200 --> 00:16:51,680
It reads as an inventory of the places

433
00:16:49,320 --> 00:16:54,160
where the chip was still waiting.

434
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:56,480
There turn out to be three.

435
00:16:54,160 --> 00:16:57,880
The first wait sits between the matrix

436
00:16:56,480 --> 00:17:00,520
multiplies.

437
00:16:57,880 --> 00:17:02,920
The online SoftMax does its bookkeeping

438
00:17:00,520 --> 00:17:04,280
between them, the rescales and the

439
00:17:02,920 --> 00:17:06,720
divisions.

440
00:17:04,280 --> 00:17:08,680
That bookkeeping runs on the CUDA Cores,

441
00:17:06,720 --> 00:17:10,319
the general-purpose scalar units from

442
00:17:08,680 --> 00:17:13,120
the last video.

443
00:17:10,319 --> 00:17:15,720
While it runs, the Tensor Cores hold.

444
00:17:13,120 --> 00:17:20,360
The cost of that holding is steep.

445
00:17:15,720 --> 00:17:22,560
On an A100, the tensor cores deliver 312

446
00:17:20,360 --> 00:17:23,640
trillion matrix multiply operations per

447
00:17:22,560 --> 00:17:26,199
second.

448
00:17:23,640 --> 00:17:28,400
The scalar floating point path delivers

449
00:17:26,199 --> 00:17:33,240
19.5 trillion.

450
00:17:28,400 --> 00:17:35,720
Divide. 312 over 19.5 is 16.

451
00:17:33,240 --> 00:17:38,320
Every scalar operation the kernel cannot

452
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:40,560
avoid costs about 16 matrix operations

453
00:17:38,320 --> 00:17:42,600
worth of machine time.

454
00:17:40,560 --> 00:17:45,080
Flash attention two rearranges the

455
00:17:42,600 --> 00:17:47,000
bookkeeping so that less of its time

456
00:17:45,080 --> 00:17:49,600
spends between one matrix multiply and

457
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:52,120
the next. The output now writes through

458
00:17:49,600 --> 00:17:54,000
the whole loop unnormalized.

459
00:17:52,120 --> 00:17:57,000
The division by the denominator happens

460
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:58,040
once at the end instead of inside every

461
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:00,240
step. [music]

462
00:17:58,040 --> 00:18:02,200
The result is unchanged and the waiting

463
00:18:00,240 --> 00:18:04,280
shrinks.

464
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:06,760
The second wait is whole multiprocessors

465
00:18:04,280 --> 00:18:09,120
sitting with no work at all. Seeing it

466
00:18:06,760 --> 00:18:10,400
takes one fact about how GPUs hand out

467
00:18:09,120 --> 00:18:12,280
work.

468
00:18:10,400 --> 00:18:14,480
A kernel is distributed as thread

469
00:18:12,280 --> 00:18:17,040
blocks, bundles of a few hundred

470
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:19,200
threads. Each block is placed on one

471
00:18:17,040 --> 00:18:20,680
streaming multiprocessor and runs to

472
00:18:19,200 --> 00:18:22,880
completion there.

473
00:18:20,680 --> 00:18:26,240
So the number of blocks is the number of

474
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:28,160
multiprocessors you can keep busy.

475
00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:30,400
The first flash attention created one

476
00:18:28,160 --> 00:18:32,800
block per head per sequence in the

477
00:18:30,400 --> 00:18:34,560
batch. The batch is however many

478
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:36,280
separate sequences the chip is

479
00:18:34,560 --> 00:18:38,560
processing at once.

480
00:18:36,280 --> 00:18:40,640
Serve a couple of long sequences and

481
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:43,840
that product is small.

482
00:18:40,640 --> 00:18:46,160
A batch of two with 16 heads makes 32

483
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:47,720
blocks on a chip with over a hundred

484
00:18:46,160 --> 00:18:49,880
multiprocessors.

485
00:18:47,720 --> 00:18:52,280
The rest sit [music] idle.

486
00:18:49,880 --> 00:18:55,400
Flash attention two adds a third source

487
00:18:52,280 --> 00:18:57,360
of blocks. It splits the query rows of a

488
00:18:55,400 --> 00:19:00,840
single long sequence across many of

489
00:18:57,360 --> 00:19:03,560
them. So one sequence alone can fan out

490
00:19:00,840 --> 00:19:05,320
and occupy the whole chip.

491
00:19:03,560 --> 00:19:07,720
The third wait is inside the thread

492
00:19:05,320 --> 00:19:09,600
block among the warps.

493
00:19:07,720 --> 00:19:12,040
Warps are the 32 thread groups from the

494
00:19:09,600 --> 00:19:14,200
last video that execute in lockstep.

495
00:19:12,040 --> 00:19:15,760
Version one divided K and V across a

496
00:19:14,200 --> 00:19:17,680
block swaps.

497
00:19:15,760 --> 00:19:19,680
That left each warp holding partial

498
00:19:17,680 --> 00:19:21,960
results it had to exchange with the

499
00:19:19,680 --> 00:19:24,640
others through the small on-chip memory

500
00:19:21,960 --> 00:19:27,160
they share before anything could finish.

501
00:19:24,640 --> 00:19:29,560
Version 2 divides Q instead.

502
00:19:27,160 --> 00:19:31,800
Now each warp carries its own rows all

503
00:19:29,560 --> 00:19:34,360
the way to the output and the exchange

504
00:19:31,800 --> 00:19:37,120
largely disappears.

505
00:19:34,360 --> 00:19:38,760
Three weights, three rearrangements.

506
00:19:37,120 --> 00:19:43,040
The number we are tracking roughly

507
00:19:38,760 --> 00:19:46,160
doubles. 25 to 40% of peak becomes 50 to

508
00:19:43,040 --> 00:19:48,600
73% on the A100.

509
00:19:46,160 --> 00:19:51,040
The cleanest end-to-end test of a kernel

510
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:53,320
is a full training run. So the headline

511
00:19:51,040 --> 00:19:55,440
figure here is a training number rather

512
00:19:53,320 --> 00:19:58,320
than an inference one.

513
00:19:55,440 --> 00:20:01,760
On that test, GPT-style models reached

514
00:19:58,320 --> 00:20:04,560
up to 225 trillion operations per second

515
00:20:01,760 --> 00:20:07,720
per A100. That works out to a model

516
00:20:04,560 --> 00:20:09,280
flops utilization of 72%.

517
00:20:07,720 --> 00:20:11,240
That figure is the fraction of the

518
00:20:09,280 --> 00:20:14,040
hardware's theoretical arithmetic the

519
00:20:11,240 --> 00:20:17,120
whole run actually used.

520
00:20:14,040 --> 00:20:19,840
Those are A100 numbers measured on the

521
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:21,800
chip the kernel was written for.

522
00:20:19,840 --> 00:20:24,800
Hopper, the architecture underneath the

523
00:20:21,800 --> 00:20:26,760
H100, changed the machine.

524
00:20:24,800 --> 00:20:28,640
The kernel's assumptions did not move

525
00:20:26,760 --> 00:20:31,560
with it.

526
00:20:28,640 --> 00:20:35,280
On the H100, this kernel reached only

527
00:20:31,560 --> 00:20:36,800
about 35% of peak down from the 50 to

528
00:20:35,280 --> 00:20:39,440
73%

529
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:42,560
it hit on the A100.

530
00:20:39,440 --> 00:20:44,880
Hopper, the architecture under the H100,

531
00:20:42,560 --> 00:20:48,080
had shipped two new features and flash

532
00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:50,760
attention 2 used neither.

533
00:20:48,080 --> 00:20:54,720
The first is a tensor core instruction

534
00:20:50,760 --> 00:20:58,240
called WGMM A. It is issued by a warp

535
00:20:54,720 --> 00:21:00,080
group, four warps acting as one.

536
00:20:58,240 --> 00:21:02,400
What makes it new is that it runs

537
00:21:00,080 --> 00:21:05,000
asynchronously. It starts a matrix

538
00:21:02,400 --> 00:21:08,120
multiply, then lets the warps move on to

539
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:10,320
other work while the multiply runs.

540
00:21:08,120 --> 00:21:13,680
The second feature we met last video,

541
00:21:10,320 --> 00:21:16,600
the tensor memory accelerator or TMA.

542
00:21:13,680 --> 00:21:19,000
The TMA is a dedicated copy engine.

543
00:21:16,600 --> 00:21:21,440
It moves tiles between HBM and the

544
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:24,640
multiprocessors fast memory entirely on

545
00:21:21,440 --> 00:21:27,080
its own with no threads required.

546
00:21:24,640 --> 00:21:29,840
Put together, these two features let the

547
00:21:27,080 --> 00:21:33,400
chip do its two jobs at the same time,

548
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:35,560
moving data and doing math.

549
00:21:33,400 --> 00:21:36,480
FlashAttention 2 did them strictly in

550
00:21:35,560 --> 00:21:39,160
turn.

551
00:21:36,480 --> 00:21:41,080
It would load a tile, compute on it,

552
00:21:39,160 --> 00:21:43,600
then load [music] the next.

553
00:21:41,080 --> 00:21:46,560
So, the copy engine sat idle during the

554
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:49,480
math, and the math sat idle during the

555
00:21:46,560 --> 00:21:51,320
copies on hardware built so neither has

556
00:21:49,480 --> 00:21:54,680
to wait.

557
00:21:51,320 --> 00:21:57,080
FlashAttention 3 published in 2024

558
00:21:54,680 --> 00:21:58,360
reorganizes the kernel around that idle

559
00:21:57,080 --> 00:22:00,000
time. [music]

560
00:21:58,360 --> 00:22:01,840
The technique is called warp

561
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:04,480
specialization.

562
00:22:01,840 --> 00:22:07,440
Instead of every warp doing every job,

563
00:22:04,480 --> 00:22:10,160
the warps divide by occupation. Producer

564
00:22:07,440 --> 00:22:13,000
warps do nothing but issue TMA loads,

565
00:22:10,160 --> 00:22:15,360
pulling the next tiles out of HBM.

566
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:17,320
Consumer warps do nothing but compute,

567
00:22:15,360 --> 00:22:19,120
running the matrix multiplies and the

568
00:22:17,320 --> 00:22:20,320
softmax on tiles that have already

569
00:22:19,120 --> 00:22:22,160
landed.

570
00:22:20,320 --> 00:22:24,440
The producers run ahead while the

571
00:22:22,160 --> 00:22:26,840
consumers work through what has arrived,

572
00:22:24,440 --> 00:22:29,840
so data movement and computation finally

573
00:22:26,840 --> 00:22:31,880
overlap, each hiding the other.

574
00:22:29,840 --> 00:22:34,040
The same overlap continues one level

575
00:22:31,880 --> 00:22:35,360
down between two separate pieces of

576
00:22:34,040 --> 00:22:38,000
silicon.

577
00:22:35,360 --> 00:22:40,450
The tensor cores run one tile's output

578
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:41,600
multiply, the attention weights times

579
00:22:40,450 --> 00:22:43,040
[music] V.

580
00:22:41,600 --> 00:22:45,000
At the same time, the next tile's

581
00:22:43,040 --> 00:22:47,480
exponentials are computed elsewhere on

582
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:50,120
the die on a third unit that is neither

583
00:22:47,480 --> 00:22:52,680
tensor core nor CUDA core.

584
00:22:50,120 --> 00:22:57,640
Nvidia calls it the special function

585
00:22:52,680 --> 00:23:00,160
unit or multi-function unit, MoFu.

586
00:22:57,640 --> 00:23:01,720
FlashAttention 3's other contribution is

587
00:23:00,160 --> 00:23:03,840
precision.

588
00:23:01,720 --> 00:23:07,080
Hopper's tensor Cores can compute in

589
00:23:03,840 --> 00:23:09,400
FP8, 8 bits per value. That runs at

590
00:23:07,080 --> 00:23:12,040
roughly double the arithmetic rate of 16

591
00:23:09,400 --> 00:23:14,920
bits, but 8 bits can only express a

592
00:23:12,040 --> 00:23:17,440
couple hundred distinct values.

593
00:23:14,920 --> 00:23:19,240
Using them means quantizing, rounding

594
00:23:17,440 --> 00:23:21,320
every number to the nearest value the

595
00:23:19,240 --> 00:23:23,200
format can express.

596
00:23:21,320 --> 00:23:24,600
That goes wrong in a specific way for

597
00:23:23,200 --> 00:23:26,920
transformers.

598
00:23:24,600 --> 00:23:29,640
Their values mostly huddle near zero

599
00:23:26,920 --> 00:23:31,920
with a handful of outliers far out. The

600
00:23:29,640 --> 00:23:34,520
format has one scale, and it must

601
00:23:31,920 --> 00:23:37,160
stretch to reach those outliers.

602
00:23:34,520 --> 00:23:39,520
Once it does, the crowded majority near

603
00:23:37,160 --> 00:23:42,080
zero lands on just a few of the

604
00:23:39,520 --> 00:23:44,640
available levels. Most of the format's

605
00:23:42,080 --> 00:23:46,160
resolution is spent on values that never

606
00:23:44,640 --> 00:23:48,840
occur.

607
00:23:46,160 --> 00:23:51,920
The kernel answers this twice.

608
00:23:48,840 --> 00:23:55,000
First, each block of values gets its own

609
00:23:51,920 --> 00:23:57,086
scale, so one region's outlier no longer

610
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:57,240
stretches everybody's.

611
00:23:57,086 --> 00:24:00,080
>> [music]

612
00:23:57,240 --> 00:24:04,040
>> Second, a trick the authors call

613
00:24:00,080 --> 00:24:06,560
incoherent processing, multiply Q and K

614
00:24:04,040 --> 00:24:07,920
by a fixed random rotation before

615
00:24:06,560 --> 00:24:10,560
quantizing.

616
00:24:07,920 --> 00:24:13,280
The rotation smears each loud coordinate

617
00:24:10,560 --> 00:24:15,800
thinly across all the dimensions, so no

618
00:24:13,280 --> 00:24:18,680
single coordinate stands out anymore,

619
00:24:15,800 --> 00:24:20,360
and the attention scores do not change.

620
00:24:18,680 --> 00:24:22,720
A rotation never changes the dot

621
00:24:20,360 --> 00:24:25,200
products between vectors, and applied to

622
00:24:22,720 --> 00:24:27,560
both Q and K, the rotations cancel

623
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:29,600
inside Q times K transpose. The

624
00:24:27,560 --> 00:24:32,080
combination cuts the numerical error by

625
00:24:29,600 --> 00:24:35,120
2.6 times.

626
00:24:32,080 --> 00:24:36,840
The payoff is 1.5 to 2 times over Flash

627
00:24:35,120 --> 00:24:40,760
Attention, too.

628
00:24:36,840 --> 00:24:43,160
In FP16, the kernel reaches up to 740

629
00:24:40,760 --> 00:24:47,600
trillion operations per second, about

630
00:24:43,160 --> 00:24:50,320
3/4 of the H100's FP16 peak.

631
00:24:47,600 --> 00:24:52,000
In FP8, it climbs close to 1.2

632
00:24:50,320 --> 00:24:54,280
quadrillion.

633
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:57,520
And on Nvidia's next chip, the Tensor

634
00:24:54,280 --> 00:24:59,680
Cores stop being the thing in the way.

635
00:24:57,520 --> 00:25:02,000
Blackwell is that chip. It is where the

636
00:24:59,680 --> 00:25:05,400
climb we have been tracking levels off,

637
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:08,520
and the reason is in the specifications.

638
00:25:05,400 --> 00:25:11,920
From the H100 to the B200, Tensor Core

639
00:25:08,520 --> 00:25:14,240
throughput in BF16, the 16-bit format,

640
00:25:11,920 --> 00:25:17,720
rises from 1 quadrillion operations per

641
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:20,680
second to 2.25 quadrillion.

642
00:25:17,720 --> 00:25:22,680
That is a factor of 2.25.

643
00:25:20,680 --> 00:25:24,960
The special function units that compute

644
00:25:22,680 --> 00:25:26,560
the exponential do not grow at all, and

645
00:25:24,960 --> 00:25:29,080
neither does the bandwidth into the

646
00:25:26,560 --> 00:25:32,240
multiprocessor's shared memory.

647
00:25:29,080 --> 00:25:34,080
When one engine gets 2.25 times faster,

648
00:25:32,240 --> 00:25:37,280
and its neighbors stay fixed, the

649
00:25:34,080 --> 00:25:39,760
bottleneck does not disappear. It moves.

650
00:25:37,280 --> 00:25:42,520
In the forward pass, the new bottleneck

651
00:25:39,760 --> 00:25:45,320
is the exponential unit. In the backward

652
00:25:42,520 --> 00:25:47,600
pass, it is shared memory.

653
00:25:45,320 --> 00:25:49,840
TriDao's group calls this asymmetric

654
00:25:47,600 --> 00:25:53,000
hardware scaling, and it changes the

655
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:55,040
job. Raising Tensor Core utilization is

656
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:57,480
no longer the lever.

657
00:25:55,040 --> 00:26:00,200
FlashAttention-4, the Blackwell kernel

658
00:25:57,480 --> 00:26:02,320
published in 2026, answers with three

659
00:26:00,200 --> 00:26:04,640
changes to the algorithm and one change

660
00:26:02,320 --> 00:26:06,160
of tools.

661
00:26:04,640 --> 00:26:08,200
The first change goes after the

662
00:26:06,160 --> 00:26:10,800
exponential itself.

663
00:26:08,200 --> 00:26:12,920
Every entry of the softmax needs one,

664
00:26:10,800 --> 00:26:15,800
and the hardware that provides it is the

665
00:26:12,920 --> 00:26:18,200
multifunction unit we met earlier. On

666
00:26:15,800 --> 00:26:19,920
Blackwell, it delivers about 16

667
00:26:18,200 --> 00:26:21,560
exponentials per cycle per

668
00:26:19,920 --> 00:26:24,600
multiprocessor.

669
00:26:21,560 --> 00:26:26,920
The BF16 Tensor Cores beside it deliver

670
00:26:24,600 --> 00:26:29,000
8,192

671
00:26:26,920 --> 00:26:32,040
operations per cycle.

672
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:34,760
Divide. 8,192

673
00:26:32,040 --> 00:26:37,000
over 16 is 512.

674
00:26:34,760 --> 00:26:39,880
So, the Tensor Cores can consume softmax

675
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:41,520
results 500 times faster than the unit

676
00:26:39,880 --> 00:26:43,400
producing them.

677
00:26:41,520 --> 00:26:46,080
FlashAttention-4 therefore computes

678
00:26:43,400 --> 00:26:48,800
exponentials down two paths at once. The

679
00:26:46,080 --> 00:26:50,640
hardware unit keeps running. Beside it,

680
00:26:48,800 --> 00:26:52,920
a short polynomial runs on the CUDA

681
00:26:50,640 --> 00:26:54,200
Cores, which would otherwise sit idle in

682
00:26:52,920 --> 00:26:56,280
this kernel.

683
00:26:54,200 --> 00:26:58,400
That polynomial is just a few multiplies

684
00:26:56,280 --> 00:27:00,320
and adds, approximating the same

685
00:26:58,400 --> 00:27:01,720
exponential to the accuracy the kernel

686
00:27:00,320 --> 00:27:04,000
needs.

687
00:27:01,720 --> 00:27:05,960
Splitting the work across both parts

688
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:07,960
increases the effective exponential

689
00:27:05,960 --> 00:27:09,960
throughput.

690
00:27:07,960 --> 00:27:11,520
The second change is to the rescaling we

691
00:27:09,960 --> 00:27:13,520
derived earlier.

692
00:27:11,520 --> 00:27:15,520
Each rise in the running maximum

693
00:27:13,520 --> 00:27:18,160
multiplies the denominator and the

694
00:27:15,520 --> 00:27:20,560
output onto the new scale.

695
00:27:18,160 --> 00:27:23,640
FlashAttention-4 makes that correction

696
00:27:20,560 --> 00:27:24,600
conditional. It sets a threshold called

697
00:27:23,640 --> 00:27:26,760
tau.

698
00:27:24,600 --> 00:27:30,320
The running maximum may drift by up to

699
00:27:26,760 --> 00:27:32,640
eight doublings, a factor of 256,

700
00:27:30,320 --> 00:27:35,000
before a rescale fires.

701
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:37,760
Smaller jumps simply ride along as

702
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:40,200
bounded drift in the accumulated values.

703
00:27:37,760 --> 00:27:42,640
At the end, everything is renormalized

704
00:27:40,200 --> 00:27:45,200
once by the true final maximum and the

705
00:27:42,640 --> 00:27:46,880
true final denominator, so the answer is

706
00:27:45,200 --> 00:27:49,280
unchanged.

707
00:27:46,880 --> 00:27:51,040
The paper describes the saved work as

708
00:27:49,280 --> 00:27:53,760
significant.

709
00:27:51,040 --> 00:27:57,560
In his Hot Chips talk, Tri Dao puts it

710
00:27:53,760 --> 00:27:59,720
at around 10 times fewer rescales.

711
00:27:57,560 --> 00:28:02,280
The third change is for the backward

712
00:27:59,720 --> 00:28:04,920
pass [music] training side work again,

713
00:28:02,280 --> 00:28:06,640
where shared memory is the new wall.

714
00:28:04,920 --> 00:28:11,160
Blackwell adds a memory this kernel

715
00:28:06,640 --> 00:28:13,520
leans on heavily, tensor memory or TMEM.

716
00:28:11,160 --> 00:28:15,880
It is about 256 kilobytes per

717
00:28:13,520 --> 00:28:17,920
multiprocessor, wired directly into the

718
00:28:15,880 --> 00:28:21,054
tensor cores.

719
00:28:17,920 --> 00:28:21,120
The TMA from Hopper is a copy engine.

720
00:28:21,054 --> 00:28:24,560
>> [music]

721
00:28:21,120 --> 00:28:26,240
>> TMEM is a place to keep data.

722
00:28:24,560 --> 00:28:28,560
FlashAttention-4 pairs neighboring

723
00:28:26,240 --> 00:28:31,520
multiprocessors in a mode NVIDIA calls

724
00:28:28,560 --> 00:28:34,000
2CTA. Two cooperating thread blocks

725
00:28:31,520 --> 00:28:35,600
drive one matrix multiply together, so a

726
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:37,120
single copy of the data they share

727
00:28:35,600 --> 00:28:38,960
serves both.

728
00:28:37,120 --> 00:28:41,200
The backward passes intermediate results

729
00:28:38,960 --> 00:28:42,840
then park in TMEM instead of crowding

730
00:28:41,200 --> 00:28:44,800
shared memory.

731
00:28:42,840 --> 00:28:47,320
Together, those moves roughly halve its

732
00:28:44,800 --> 00:28:49,720
shared memory traffic.

733
00:28:47,320 --> 00:28:52,360
That leaves the change of tools.

734
00:28:49,720 --> 00:28:55,600
Flash Attention 4 is written entirely in

735
00:28:52,360 --> 00:28:57,240
QDSL, Nvidia's Python embedded language

736
00:28:55,600 --> 00:28:59,720
for kernels.

737
00:28:57,240 --> 00:29:02,760
It compiles 20 to 30 times faster than

738
00:28:59,720 --> 00:29:04,960
the equivalent C++ templates. A kernel

739
00:29:02,760 --> 00:29:07,080
at this level is tuned by rebuilding it

740
00:29:04,960 --> 00:29:08,360
and measuring again hundreds of times

741
00:29:07,080 --> 00:29:10,160
over.

742
00:29:08,360 --> 00:29:13,760
So, the speed of the compiler becomes

743
00:29:10,160 --> 00:29:16,080
the speed of every experiment.

744
00:29:13,760 --> 00:29:18,880
The gains [music] are real, but regime

745
00:29:16,080 --> 00:29:23,000
specific. The paper claims the win for

746
00:29:18,880 --> 00:29:27,000
sequences of 4,000 tokens and longer.

747
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:31,640
There, the BF16 forward pass runs 1.1 to

748
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:34,680
1.3 times faster than cuDNN 9.13,

749
00:29:31,640 --> 00:29:38,240
Nvidia's own attention library, and 2.1

750
00:29:34,680 --> 00:29:39,880
to 2.7 times faster than Triton,

751
00:29:38,240 --> 00:29:41,960
the open source language much of the

752
00:29:39,880 --> 00:29:44,520
field writes kernels in.

753
00:29:41,960 --> 00:29:47,280
Below 4,000 tokens, the paper does not

754
00:29:44,520 --> 00:29:49,720
claim a consistent lead.

755
00:29:47,280 --> 00:29:53,200
Measured the same way, Flash Attention 4

756
00:29:49,720 --> 00:29:55,680
reaches up to about 1,605

757
00:29:53,200 --> 00:30:00,360
trillion operations per second. That is

758
00:29:55,680 --> 00:30:03,320
roughly 71% of the B200's BF16 peak.

759
00:30:00,360 --> 00:30:05,560
Set it beside Flash Attention 3's 75% on

760
00:30:03,320 --> 00:30:07,680
Hopper, and the fraction has plateaued

761
00:30:05,560 --> 00:30:10,600
near three quarters. The ceiling

762
00:30:07,680 --> 00:30:12,400
underneath it grew 2.25 times, yet the

763
00:30:10,600 --> 00:30:13,960
fraction held.

764
00:30:12,400 --> 00:30:15,760
The fraction holds because of the same

765
00:30:13,960 --> 00:30:18,240
three costs.

766
00:30:15,760 --> 00:30:21,120
The exponentials, the rescales, and the

767
00:30:18,240 --> 00:30:23,160
backward passes bytes.

768
00:30:21,120 --> 00:30:25,120
Every headline number here was measured

769
00:30:23,160 --> 00:30:27,280
in BF16.

770
00:30:25,120 --> 00:30:30,000
The width of the numbers is a lever we

771
00:30:27,280 --> 00:30:31,600
will return to at the end.

772
00:30:30,000 --> 00:30:33,400
Before that comes a different byte

773
00:30:31,600 --> 00:30:35,360
problem, because the kernel's

774
00:30:33,400 --> 00:30:36,960
intermediates were never the only thing

775
00:30:35,360 --> 00:30:39,320
in HBM.

776
00:30:36,960 --> 00:30:41,640
Every active request also keeps its keys

777
00:30:39,320 --> 00:30:43,480
and values there, the KV cache from the

778
00:30:41,640 --> 00:30:45,880
first video.

779
00:30:43,480 --> 00:30:48,200
The way serving systems laid that cache

780
00:30:45,880 --> 00:30:50,080
out turned out to waste more than half

781
00:30:48,200 --> 00:30:52,640
of the most expensive memory in the

782
00:30:50,080 --> 00:30:52,640
machine.

783
00:30:54,960 --> 00:30:58,840
The cache problem starts with a

784
00:30:56,360 --> 00:31:00,880
reasonable looking decision.

785
00:30:58,840 --> 00:31:03,280
A serving system cannot know in advance

786
00:31:00,880 --> 00:31:05,960
how long a response will run.

787
00:31:03,280 --> 00:31:07,320
So, the systems before 2023 played it

788
00:31:05,960 --> 00:31:09,760
safe.

789
00:31:07,320 --> 00:31:12,800
For every request, they reserved one

790
00:31:09,760 --> 00:31:14,840
contiguous stretch of HBM sized for the

791
00:31:12,800 --> 00:31:16,400
longest sequence that request might

792
00:31:14,840 --> 00:31:19,160
reach.

793
00:31:16,400 --> 00:31:22,320
Suppose the limit is 4,096

794
00:31:19,160 --> 00:31:24,800
tokens and the response stops at 200.

795
00:31:22,320 --> 00:31:27,200
The other 3,896

796
00:31:24,800 --> 00:31:28,960
slots sit reserved and empty until the

797
00:31:27,200 --> 00:31:31,080
request finishes.

798
00:31:28,960 --> 00:31:33,680
That waste inside a reservation is

799
00:31:31,080 --> 00:31:35,640
internal fragmentation.

800
00:31:33,680 --> 00:31:38,200
There is a second kind.

801
00:31:35,640 --> 00:31:40,560
As requests of different lengths start

802
00:31:38,200 --> 00:31:43,040
and finish, the free memory between the

803
00:31:40,560 --> 00:31:45,320
reservations turns ragged.

804
00:31:43,040 --> 00:31:47,840
A new request can fit comfortably in the

805
00:31:45,320 --> 00:31:50,080
total free space and still find no

806
00:31:47,840 --> 00:31:52,280
single contiguous stretch large enough

807
00:31:50,080 --> 00:31:54,240
to hold it. That is external

808
00:31:52,280 --> 00:31:56,280
fragmentation.

809
00:31:54,240 --> 00:31:59,920
Operating systems textbooks have

810
00:31:56,280 --> 00:32:02,800
described both since the 1960s.

811
00:31:59,920 --> 00:32:04,120
The VLLM team measured what the two cost

812
00:32:02,800 --> 00:32:06,680
together.

813
00:32:04,120 --> 00:32:10,120
In the serving systems of the time, only

814
00:32:06,680 --> 00:32:13,240
20.4% to 38.2%

815
00:32:10,120 --> 00:32:14,400
of the KV cache memory held actual token

816
00:32:13,240 --> 00:32:16,280
states.

817
00:32:14,400 --> 00:32:18,800
More than half of the most expensive

818
00:32:16,280 --> 00:32:20,600
memory in the machine was storing

819
00:32:18,800 --> 00:32:22,320
nothing.

820
00:32:20,600 --> 00:32:24,600
Operating systems also solved this

821
00:32:22,320 --> 00:32:27,760
problem decades ago.

822
00:32:24,600 --> 00:32:29,960
Paged attention, published in 2023,

823
00:32:27,760 --> 00:32:31,320
carries that solution over almost

824
00:32:29,960 --> 00:32:33,800
unchanged.

825
00:32:31,320 --> 00:32:36,680
Break the cache into fixed-size blocks,

826
00:32:33,800 --> 00:32:39,280
16 or 32 tokens each.

827
00:32:36,680 --> 00:32:41,320
Give every request a block table. The

828
00:32:39,280 --> 00:32:44,000
table maps its logical blocks to

829
00:32:41,320 --> 00:32:46,880
physical block identifiers.

830
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:49,000
Physically, a request's blocks can sit

831
00:32:46,880 --> 00:32:51,360
anywhere in HBM.

832
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:53,560
Logically, the attention kernel follows

833
00:32:51,360 --> 00:32:56,400
the table and sees one contiguous

834
00:32:53,560 --> 00:32:58,920
sequence. Now, internal fragmentation

835
00:32:56,400 --> 00:33:00,120
shrinks to at most one partial block per

836
00:32:58,920 --> 00:33:02,480
request.

837
00:33:00,120 --> 00:33:03,362
And external fragmentation disappears

838
00:33:02,480 --> 00:33:03,480
entirely

839
00:33:03,362 --> 00:33:05,560
>> [music]

840
00:33:03,480 --> 00:33:08,840
>> because every free block is the same

841
00:33:05,560 --> 00:33:10,600
size and any of them will do.

842
00:33:08,840 --> 00:33:11,960
The attention kernel still computes the

843
00:33:10,600 --> 00:33:14,200
same function.

844
00:33:11,960 --> 00:33:15,560
It just gathers each block by following

845
00:33:14,200 --> 00:33:17,960
the table.

846
00:33:15,560 --> 00:33:19,760
What is new is where the bytes live,

847
00:33:17,960 --> 00:33:21,440
plus the block table indirection needed

848
00:33:19,760 --> 00:33:24,040
to find them. [music] The reclaimed

849
00:33:21,440 --> 00:33:26,560
memory turns directly into throughput.

850
00:33:24,040 --> 00:33:29,840
More concurrent requests now fit in the

851
00:33:26,560 --> 00:33:31,400
same HBM. So, vLLM serves two to four

852
00:33:29,840 --> 00:33:33,680
times the throughput of the serving

853
00:33:31,400 --> 00:33:35,680
systems that came before it at the same

854
00:33:33,680 --> 00:33:38,880
latency.

855
00:33:35,680 --> 00:33:40,680
The block table buys one more thing.

856
00:33:38,880 --> 00:33:43,160
Sometimes many sequences begin

857
00:33:40,680 --> 00:33:45,320
identically, sharing a system prompt or

858
00:33:43,160 --> 00:33:47,360
a long instruction preamble.

859
00:33:45,320 --> 00:33:49,720
Sometimes the server deliberately

860
00:33:47,360 --> 00:33:52,520
explores several candidate continuations

861
00:33:49,720 --> 00:33:54,160
of one prompt at once, a strategy called

862
00:33:52,520 --> 00:33:56,480
beam search.

863
00:33:54,160 --> 00:33:58,440
In both cases, their tables can point at

864
00:33:56,480 --> 00:34:00,320
the same physical blocks.

865
00:33:58,440 --> 00:34:02,840
A block is copied only when a sequence

866
00:34:00,320 --> 00:34:04,800
diverges and writes into it. On beam

867
00:34:02,840 --> 00:34:07,640
search, the shared portions reach up to

868
00:34:04,800 --> 00:34:10,120
55% of the cache.

869
00:34:07,640 --> 00:34:11,960
That saved memory feeds straight back

870
00:34:10,120 --> 00:34:15,639
into batch size.

871
00:34:11,960 --> 00:34:17,600
In all, vLLM's throughput runs from 1.3

872
00:34:15,639 --> 00:34:20,600
times the previous best system on plain

873
00:34:17,600 --> 00:34:22,840
sampling to 2.3 times on beam search six

874
00:34:20,600 --> 00:34:24,840
candidates wide.

875
00:34:22,840 --> 00:34:26,399
Paging controls where the cached bytes

876
00:34:24,840 --> 00:34:28,120
are placed.

877
00:34:26,399 --> 00:34:30,360
How many of them there need to be is a

878
00:34:28,120 --> 00:34:32,639
separate lever, and that one is

879
00:34:30,360 --> 00:34:35,080
architectural.

880
00:34:32,639 --> 00:34:37,720
In standard multi-head attention, every

881
00:34:35,080 --> 00:34:39,679
head carries its own keys and values.

882
00:34:37,720 --> 00:34:42,960
So, the cache stores two vectors per

883
00:34:39,679 --> 00:34:45,040
head per layer for every token. Write

884
00:34:42,960 --> 00:34:47,600
that out as a formula.

885
00:34:45,040 --> 00:34:50,520
Two times the head count times the head

886
00:34:47,600 --> 00:34:52,639
dimension times the number of layers.

887
00:34:50,520 --> 00:34:54,840
The first video priced that formula out

888
00:34:52,639 --> 00:34:57,320
for Llama 370B.

889
00:34:54,840 --> 00:34:59,920
It came to 320 kilobytes for every

890
00:34:57,320 --> 00:35:02,120
token. Hundreds of megabytes for one

891
00:34:59,920 --> 00:35:04,120
long conversation. And that figure

892
00:35:02,120 --> 00:35:06,720
already includes the optimization we are

893
00:35:04,120 --> 00:35:08,680
coming to. Without it, the bill is eight

894
00:35:06,720 --> 00:35:11,800
times larger.

895
00:35:08,680 --> 00:35:15,040
In 2019, Noam Shazeer noticed where the

896
00:35:11,800 --> 00:35:17,480
bill lands. Generating each token means

897
00:35:15,040 --> 00:35:19,440
reloading all of those cached keys and

898
00:35:17,480 --> 00:35:21,920
values from HBM.

899
00:35:19,440 --> 00:35:24,320
That reloading is bandwidth bound, the

900
00:35:21,920 --> 00:35:27,080
same constraint behind every technique

901
00:35:24,320 --> 00:35:29,160
so far. And most of those bytes exist

902
00:35:27,080 --> 00:35:31,160
only because every head insists on a

903
00:35:29,160 --> 00:35:32,920
private copy.

904
00:35:31,160 --> 00:35:34,320
His proposal is called multi-query

905
00:35:32,920 --> 00:35:36,120
attention.

906
00:35:34,320 --> 00:35:38,520
Keep all the query heads, but let them

907
00:35:36,120 --> 00:35:40,120
share one single key head and one single

908
00:35:38,520 --> 00:35:41,520
value head.

909
00:35:40,120 --> 00:35:43,840
Now, the head count term in the cache

910
00:35:41,520 --> 00:35:45,400
formula drops to one. The cache shrinks

911
00:35:43,840 --> 00:35:47,640
by the full head count, and the

912
00:35:45,400 --> 00:35:49,840
bandwidth bill shrinks with it. Models

913
00:35:47,640 --> 00:35:51,080
like Falcon and Palm shipped exactly

914
00:35:49,840 --> 00:35:53,200
this.

915
00:35:51,080 --> 00:35:57,360
The price is quality.

916
00:35:53,200 --> 00:35:59,640
64 query heads were trained to ask 64

917
00:35:57,360 --> 00:36:02,200
different kinds of questions.

918
00:35:59,640 --> 00:36:04,520
Under multi-query, every one of them now

919
00:36:02,200 --> 00:36:06,120
consults the same single description of

920
00:36:04,520 --> 00:36:09,280
each token.

921
00:36:06,120 --> 00:36:12,600
Accuracy measurably drops.

922
00:36:09,280 --> 00:36:15,280
Grouped query attention from 2023 turns

923
00:36:12,600 --> 00:36:17,560
that all-or-nothing choice into a dial.

924
00:36:15,280 --> 00:36:19,880
Split the query heads into groups and

925
00:36:17,560 --> 00:36:21,200
give each group its own key and value

926
00:36:19,880 --> 00:36:23,240
head.

927
00:36:21,200 --> 00:36:25,320
One group is multi-query, and one group

928
00:36:23,240 --> 00:36:27,720
per head is full multi-head. So, the

929
00:36:25,320 --> 00:36:29,640
useful settings sit in between. Eight

930
00:36:27,720 --> 00:36:31,920
groups turns out to hold quality close

931
00:36:29,640 --> 00:36:33,160
to multi-head at close to multi-query

932
00:36:31,920 --> 00:36:35,560
speed.

933
00:36:33,160 --> 00:36:38,640
An existing multi-head model can even be

934
00:36:35,560 --> 00:36:40,825
converted to this layout for about 5% of

935
00:36:38,640 --> 00:36:40,960
its original pre-training compute.

936
00:36:40,825 --> 00:36:43,480
>> [music]

937
00:36:40,960 --> 00:36:46,080
>> The paper calls that up-training.

938
00:36:43,480 --> 00:36:48,800
That combination is why Llama 2, Llama

939
00:36:46,080 --> 00:36:50,760
3, and Mistral adopted it almost

940
00:36:48,800 --> 00:36:54,120
immediately.

941
00:36:50,760 --> 00:36:56,000
Sharing heads is one way to store less.

942
00:36:54,120 --> 00:36:59,240
DeepSeek took a different route,

943
00:36:56,000 --> 00:37:02,840
compressing what each token stores.

944
00:36:59,240 --> 00:37:05,760
DeepSeek V2, from May 2024, cuts the

945
00:37:02,840 --> 00:37:07,640
cache far deeper than grouping can.

946
00:37:05,760 --> 00:37:09,200
It does this by changing what gets

947
00:37:07,640 --> 00:37:11,240
stored.

948
00:37:09,200 --> 00:37:14,080
Multi-head latent attention does not

949
00:37:11,240 --> 00:37:16,560
keep keys and values at all. For each

950
00:37:14,080 --> 00:37:18,080
token, it stores one short vector called

951
00:37:16,560 --> 00:37:20,480
a latent.

952
00:37:18,080 --> 00:37:22,840
The latent is a compressed summary that

953
00:37:20,480 --> 00:37:25,680
the model itself learns to write during

954
00:37:22,840 --> 00:37:27,760
training. From that summary, the keys

955
00:37:25,680 --> 00:37:29,600
and values can be rebuilt at the moment

956
00:37:27,760 --> 00:37:32,240
attention needs them.

957
00:37:29,600 --> 00:37:34,240
The paper calls this low-rank joint

958
00:37:32,240 --> 00:37:37,800
compression.

959
00:37:34,240 --> 00:37:40,640
In DeepSeek V2, there are 128 heads,

960
00:37:37,800 --> 00:37:42,440
each of dimension 128.

961
00:37:40,640 --> 00:37:45,080
That makes the full keys and values

962
00:37:42,440 --> 00:37:48,080
32,768

963
00:37:45,080 --> 00:37:51,840
elements per layer for every token.

964
00:37:48,080 --> 00:37:54,800
The latent stores 512 elements.

965
00:37:51,840 --> 00:37:57,560
A small 64-element companion rides

966
00:37:54,800 --> 00:38:01,080
beside it to carry position, and we will

967
00:37:57,560 --> 00:38:06,160
come to that in a moment. So, 576

968
00:38:01,080 --> 00:38:06,160
elements are stored in place of 32,768.

969
00:38:06,760 --> 00:38:10,920
Divide.

970
00:38:08,040 --> 00:38:12,920
32,768

971
00:38:10,920 --> 00:38:14,920
over 576

972
00:38:12,920 --> 00:38:17,320
is about 57.

973
00:38:14,920 --> 00:38:19,040
The cache is 57 times smaller. The

974
00:38:17,320 --> 00:38:21,760
footprint of a grouped query model with

975
00:38:19,040 --> 00:38:24,320
just 2.25 groups, and nothing is being

976
00:38:21,760 --> 00:38:26,800
discarded. The full keys and values are

977
00:38:24,320 --> 00:38:29,280
rebuilt from the summary on the fly by

978
00:38:26,800 --> 00:38:32,120
two learned decompression matrices only

979
00:38:29,280 --> 00:38:34,200
for the tokens being attended to.

980
00:38:32,120 --> 00:38:36,400
Rebuilding them costs extra matrix

981
00:38:34,200 --> 00:38:39,080
multiplies on every step.

982
00:38:36,400 --> 00:38:41,600
Arithmetic is cheap. HBM bytes are

983
00:38:39,080 --> 00:38:44,120
expensive, and decode leaves the tensor

984
00:38:41,600 --> 00:38:46,400
cores mostly idle anyway.

985
00:38:44,120 --> 00:38:48,760
MLA spends that idle arithmetic to buy

986
00:38:46,400 --> 00:38:50,640
back cache space and the bandwidth to

987
00:38:48,760 --> 00:38:53,080
read it.

988
00:38:50,640 --> 00:38:54,480
In practice, even the extra is largely

989
00:38:53,080 --> 00:38:56,840
vanish.

990
00:38:54,480 --> 00:38:59,600
Two matrix multiplies in a row are the

991
00:38:56,840 --> 00:39:02,200
same as one multiplied by their product.

992
00:38:59,600 --> 00:39:05,800
A query only ever meets a reconstructed

993
00:39:02,200 --> 00:39:08,560
key inside a chain of matrix multiplies.

994
00:39:05,800 --> 00:39:11,040
So, the decompression matrix for keys

995
00:39:08,560 --> 00:39:13,360
can fold into the matrix that produces

996
00:39:11,040 --> 00:39:16,200
queries, and the decompression matrix

997
00:39:13,360 --> 00:39:18,960
for values into the matrix that produces

998
00:39:16,200 --> 00:39:20,600
the output. The kernel simply reorders

999
00:39:18,960 --> 00:39:23,440
its multiplies.

1000
00:39:20,600 --> 00:39:26,520
No step ever rebuilds the full keys and

1001
00:39:23,440 --> 00:39:28,200
values from the cached latents at all.

1002
00:39:26,520 --> 00:39:32,120
The decompression dissolves into

1003
00:39:28,200 --> 00:39:34,720
multiplies the model was already doing.

1004
00:39:32,120 --> 00:39:37,080
One thing nearly breaks this.

1005
00:39:34,720 --> 00:39:39,800
Transformers encode each token's

1006
00:39:37,080 --> 00:39:42,680
position by rotating its query and key

1007
00:39:39,800 --> 00:39:45,920
vectors by a position dependent angle, a

1008
00:39:42,680 --> 00:39:47,920
scheme called rotary position embedding.

1009
00:39:45,920 --> 00:39:50,440
That rotation changes from token to

1010
00:39:47,920 --> 00:39:52,360
token, so it cannot be merged into any

1011
00:39:50,440 --> 00:39:54,480
fixed matrix.

1012
00:39:52,360 --> 00:39:56,720
Applied to a reconstructed key, [music]

1013
00:39:54,480 --> 00:39:58,360
and it wedges an unmergeable step into

1014
00:39:56,720 --> 00:40:00,960
the middle of the chain.

1015
00:39:58,360 --> 00:40:02,800
DeepSeek's answer is to route position

1016
00:40:00,960 --> 00:40:06,160
around the compression.

1017
00:40:02,800 --> 00:40:08,960
A small 64-element channel rides beside

1018
00:40:06,160 --> 00:40:10,600
the latent and carries the rotated part.

1019
00:40:08,960 --> 00:40:13,120
The 512-element

1020
00:40:10,600 --> 00:40:15,000
summary stays position free, so the

1021
00:40:13,120 --> 00:40:17,280
merge stays legal.

1022
00:40:15,000 --> 00:40:20,360
That channel is the companion we counted

1023
00:40:17,280 --> 00:40:22,080
into the 576.

1024
00:40:20,360 --> 00:40:25,240
Measured against Deep Seek's own

1025
00:40:22,080 --> 00:40:28,480
previous 67 billion parameter model, the

1026
00:40:25,240 --> 00:40:30,800
KV cache shrinks by 93.3%

1027
00:40:28,480 --> 00:40:33,440
and maximum generation throughput rises

1028
00:40:30,800 --> 00:40:36,200
to 5.76 times.

1029
00:40:33,440 --> 00:40:38,480
They also report quality at or above

1030
00:40:36,200 --> 00:40:41,160
full multi-head attention.

1031
00:40:38,480 --> 00:40:42,480
That last claim is one to read carefully

1032
00:40:41,160 --> 00:40:44,480
since it comes from their own

1033
00:40:42,480 --> 00:40:46,360
comparisons inside their own training

1034
00:40:44,480 --> 00:40:49,120
recipe.

1035
00:40:46,360 --> 00:40:51,160
Paged, grouped, and compressed, the

1036
00:40:49,120 --> 00:40:52,800
cache is now a fraction of its former

1037
00:40:51,160 --> 00:40:54,560
size.

1038
00:40:52,800 --> 00:40:56,480
Shrinking the cache does not change how

1039
00:40:54,560 --> 00:40:58,680
the cache is read though.

1040
00:40:56,480 --> 00:41:00,720
And at decode, the reading has a problem

1041
00:40:58,680 --> 00:41:03,200
of its own. The way Flash Attention

1042
00:41:00,720 --> 00:41:06,040
divides its work collapses when the

1043
00:41:03,200 --> 00:41:08,080
query is a single token.

1044
00:41:06,040 --> 00:41:09,800
Flash Attention 2 fills a chip by

1045
00:41:08,080 --> 00:41:12,000
handing out many independent blocks of

1046
00:41:09,800 --> 00:41:14,680
work, and each block is one bundle of

1047
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:16,120
query rows for one head for one sequence

1048
00:41:14,680 --> 00:41:20,200
in the batch.

1049
00:41:16,120 --> 00:41:21,880
The H100 has 132 multiprocessors, and

1050
00:41:20,200 --> 00:41:23,600
the number of blocks is the number it

1051
00:41:21,880 --> 00:41:26,280
can keep busy.

1052
00:41:23,600 --> 00:41:28,640
Prefill produces thousands of blocks,

1053
00:41:26,280 --> 00:41:31,560
more than enough to go around.

1054
00:41:28,640 --> 00:41:34,320
Decode produces almost none.

1055
00:41:31,560 --> 00:41:36,520
The query at decode is one new token, a

1056
00:41:34,320 --> 00:41:39,360
single row. So, the query dimension

1057
00:41:36,520 --> 00:41:41,440
contributes exactly one block. The block

1058
00:41:39,360 --> 00:41:42,760
count collapses to the batch size times

1059
00:41:41,440 --> 00:41:45,320
the head count.

1060
00:41:42,760 --> 00:41:48,120
Serve one request on a model with 32

1061
00:41:45,320 --> 00:41:49,520
heads, and the chip receives 32 pieces

1062
00:41:48,120 --> 00:41:53,080
of work.

1063
00:41:49,520 --> 00:41:55,400
100 multiprocessors stand idle.

1064
00:41:53,080 --> 00:41:57,920
Each busy one walks the entire cached

1065
00:41:55,400 --> 00:41:59,800
sequence alone, tile after tile, and the

1066
00:41:57,920 --> 00:42:02,120
longer the context, the longer that

1067
00:41:59,800 --> 00:42:04,160
serial walk.

1068
00:42:02,120 --> 00:42:07,720
The work is all in one dimension that

1069
00:42:04,160 --> 00:42:09,760
nobody had split, the cache itself.

1070
00:42:07,720 --> 00:42:12,760
Flash Decoding, published in October

1071
00:42:09,760 --> 00:42:15,080
2023 by Tri Dao and collaborators,

1072
00:42:12,760 --> 00:42:16,280
splits the cached keys and values into

1073
00:42:15,080 --> 00:42:19,280
chunks.

1074
00:42:16,280 --> 00:42:22,040
Every chunk gets its own block of work,

1075
00:42:19,280 --> 00:42:23,960
which means its own multi-processor.

1076
00:42:22,040 --> 00:42:26,680
Each one computes the new tokens

1077
00:42:23,960 --> 00:42:29,840
attention against its own chunk as if

1078
00:42:26,680 --> 00:42:32,480
that chunk were the whole sequence.

1079
00:42:29,840 --> 00:42:34,880
SoftMax should forbid that.

1080
00:42:32,480 --> 00:42:37,560
Its normalization couples every position

1081
00:42:34,880 --> 00:42:40,360
in a row to every other, so the partial

1082
00:42:37,560 --> 00:42:42,400
results cannot simply be added. What

1083
00:42:40,360 --> 00:42:45,600
makes the split is the bookkeeping

1084
00:42:42,400 --> 00:42:48,680
online SoftMax already keeps per row.

1085
00:42:45,600 --> 00:42:51,720
Each chunk keeps its own running maximum

1086
00:42:48,680 --> 00:42:54,560
and its own running sum of exponentials.

1087
00:42:51,720 --> 00:42:56,680
A small second kernel merges the partial

1088
00:42:54,560 --> 00:42:59,400
outputs at the end.

1089
00:42:56,680 --> 00:43:02,240
It rescales each one by how far its

1090
00:42:59,400 --> 00:43:04,800
local maximum sits below the global one.

1091
00:43:02,240 --> 00:43:07,520
The same correction online SoftMax

1092
00:43:04,800 --> 00:43:10,480
applies down a single row.

1093
00:43:07,520 --> 00:43:12,440
The merged answer is exact attention.

1094
00:43:10,480 --> 00:43:15,080
Online SoftMax ran that bookkeeping

1095
00:43:12,440 --> 00:43:17,320
serially down one row. Flash decoding

1096
00:43:15,080 --> 00:43:19,280
runs it in parallel across the chunks of

1097
00:43:17,320 --> 00:43:21,560
the cache.

1098
00:43:19,280 --> 00:43:25,960
The published benchmark runs CodeLlama

1099
00:43:21,560 --> 00:43:27,920
34B on prompts from 512 tokens out to

1100
00:43:25,960 --> 00:43:29,840
64,000.

1101
00:43:27,920 --> 00:43:33,160
Every attention implementation in the

1102
00:43:29,840 --> 00:43:35,120
comparison slows as the context grows.

1103
00:43:33,160 --> 00:43:37,720
This one does not.

1104
00:43:35,120 --> 00:43:40,400
With flash decoding, sequence length has

1105
00:43:37,720 --> 00:43:42,680
little impact on generation speed.

1106
00:43:40,400 --> 00:43:44,840
At the long end, decoding runs up to

1107
00:43:42,680 --> 00:43:46,880
eight times faster on the back of an

1108
00:43:44,840 --> 00:43:50,240
attention operation up to 50 times

1109
00:43:46,880 --> 00:43:52,120
faster than unsplit flash attention.

1110
00:43:50,240 --> 00:43:54,840
The gain concentrates exactly where

1111
00:43:52,120 --> 00:43:57,280
decode hurt most, at long context and

1112
00:43:54,840 --> 00:43:59,240
small batch. A large batch already fills

1113
00:43:57,280 --> 00:44:01,080
the chip through the batch dimension by

1114
00:43:59,240 --> 00:44:03,760
the same block counting argument from

1115
00:44:01,080 --> 00:44:06,400
flash attention, too.

1116
00:44:03,760 --> 00:44:09,040
The cache is paged, shrunk, and now read

1117
00:44:06,400 --> 00:44:11,960
by the whole chip at once.

1118
00:44:09,040 --> 00:44:14,560
One cost at decode is still untouched.

1119
00:44:11,960 --> 00:44:18,240
The number of separate kernels the CPU

1120
00:44:14,560 --> 00:44:20,920
must launch to produce one token.

1121
00:44:18,240 --> 00:44:24,840
Producing a single token at batch size

1122
00:44:20,920 --> 00:44:28,360
one runs a long chain of small kernels.

1123
00:44:24,840 --> 00:44:31,160
Each layer launches a handful of them.

1124
00:44:28,360 --> 00:44:33,720
One makes the queries, keys, and values.

1125
00:44:31,160 --> 00:44:36,480
One applies the position rotation. One

1126
00:44:33,720 --> 00:44:38,320
runs the attention itself. One combines

1127
00:44:36,480 --> 00:44:40,320
the heads outputs.

1128
00:44:38,320 --> 00:44:42,720
And two handle the feed forward networks

1129
00:44:40,320 --> 00:44:44,680
big matrix multiplies.

1130
00:44:42,720 --> 00:44:47,240
A few more do the small add and

1131
00:44:44,680 --> 00:44:50,040
re-normalize steps between them. Across

1132
00:44:47,240 --> 00:44:52,240
a 32-layer model, that is hundreds of

1133
00:44:50,040 --> 00:44:54,040
kernel launches for every generated

1134
00:44:52,240 --> 00:44:56,480
token.

1135
00:44:54,040 --> 00:44:59,160
Each launch costs the CPU a few

1136
00:44:56,480 --> 00:45:01,560
microseconds of bookkeeping. Validating

1137
00:44:59,160 --> 00:45:03,120
arguments, queuing the work, talking to

1138
00:45:01,560 --> 00:45:05,040
the driver.

1139
00:45:03,120 --> 00:45:07,120
A few microseconds would not normally

1140
00:45:05,040 --> 00:45:09,240
matter. But at batch size one, the

1141
00:45:07,120 --> 00:45:11,240
kernels themselves often run for only a

1142
00:45:09,240 --> 00:45:13,696
few microseconds, too.

1143
00:45:11,240 --> 00:45:16,280
So, the GPU finishes each piece of work,

1144
00:45:13,696 --> 00:45:18,920
[music] and then idles, waiting for the

1145
00:45:16,280 --> 00:45:21,400
CPU to hand it the next one.

1146
00:45:18,920 --> 00:45:25,240
At small batch, that dispatch overhead

1147
00:45:21,400 --> 00:45:27,520
can reach 20 to 30% of the whole step.

1148
00:45:25,240 --> 00:45:30,040
CUDA graphs amortize that overhead

1149
00:45:27,520 --> 00:45:32,000
instead of paying it per kernel.

1150
00:45:30,040 --> 00:45:34,480
During a warm-up step, the runtime

1151
00:45:32,000 --> 00:45:37,440
records the entire launch sequence into

1152
00:45:34,480 --> 00:45:39,960
a graph object. From then on, a single

1153
00:45:37,440 --> 00:45:41,840
call replays the whole graph. Hundreds

1154
00:45:39,960 --> 00:45:43,480
of kernels are dispatched for the price

1155
00:45:41,840 --> 00:45:45,360
of one.

1156
00:45:43,480 --> 00:45:47,480
The one constraint is that a replayed

1157
00:45:45,360 --> 00:45:50,000
graph must touch the same memory

1158
00:45:47,480 --> 00:45:52,440
addresses every time, which is awkward

1159
00:45:50,000 --> 00:45:54,520
for a paged cache. So, serving systems

1160
00:45:52,440 --> 00:45:57,200
lay out fixed buffers to keep their

1161
00:45:54,520 --> 00:45:59,760
decode graphs replayable.

1162
00:45:57,200 --> 00:46:01,360
Prefill runs a few large kernels and

1163
00:45:59,760 --> 00:46:04,600
gains little.

1164
00:46:01,360 --> 00:46:06,880
Decode at small batch gains the most,

1165
00:46:04,600 --> 00:46:10,840
which is why the major serving systems

1166
00:46:06,880 --> 00:46:12,920
all replay their decode steps this way.

1167
00:46:10,840 --> 00:46:16,120
Let's recap the levers: kernel

1168
00:46:12,920 --> 00:46:18,280
intermediates, cache layout, cache size,

1169
00:46:16,120 --> 00:46:19,640
parallel cache reads, and now launch

1170
00:46:18,280 --> 00:46:22,360
overhead.

1171
00:46:19,640 --> 00:46:24,600
Each one made the bytes cheaper to move

1172
00:46:22,360 --> 00:46:26,360
or rarer to need.

1173
00:46:24,600 --> 00:46:29,120
One lever remains, the one

1174
00:46:26,360 --> 00:46:30,640
FlashAttention-4's BF16 headline already

1175
00:46:29,120 --> 00:46:33,880
hinted at.

1176
00:46:30,640 --> 00:46:35,960
The width of each number itself.

1177
00:46:33,880 --> 00:46:37,840
This last lever follows the same logic

1178
00:46:35,960 --> 00:46:40,880
as the rest.

1179
00:46:37,840 --> 00:46:43,400
Wall clock time tracks bytes moved. So,

1180
00:46:40,880 --> 00:46:45,080
cutting each number from 16 bits to

1181
00:46:43,400 --> 00:46:46,280
eight halves the memory traffic

1182
00:46:45,080 --> 00:46:48,560
outright. [music]

1183
00:46:46,280 --> 00:46:51,160
The compute gets cheaper, too, because

1184
00:46:48,560 --> 00:46:53,480
fewer bits per operand means more Tensor

1185
00:46:51,160 --> 00:46:56,040
Core operations fit in each cycle, and

1186
00:46:53,480 --> 00:46:58,840
the hardware keeps lowering the floor.

1187
00:46:56,040 --> 00:47:01,600
Hopper's Tensor Cores run FP8, the 8-bit

1188
00:46:58,840 --> 00:47:03,360
format FlashAttention-3 exploited.

1189
00:47:01,600 --> 00:47:06,640
Blackwell's Tensor Cores reach further

1190
00:47:03,360 --> 00:47:08,760
down to 4-bit FP4.

1191
00:47:06,640 --> 00:47:12,680
Yet, FlashAttention-4's headline numbers

1192
00:47:08,760 --> 00:47:14,520
still run in 16-bit BF16.

1193
00:47:12,680 --> 00:47:16,960
The reason is that accuracy gives out

1194
00:47:14,520 --> 00:47:19,520
before the hardware floor is reached.

1195
00:47:16,960 --> 00:47:20,960
Even at 8 bits, holding the error down

1196
00:47:19,520 --> 00:47:22,680
took everything we saw in

1197
00:47:20,960 --> 00:47:25,200
FlashAttention-3.

1198
00:47:22,680 --> 00:47:27,040
A separate scale for every block, and

1199
00:47:25,200 --> 00:47:29,120
outliers scattered through random

1200
00:47:27,040 --> 00:47:31,600
rotations.

1201
00:47:29,120 --> 00:47:34,200
So, lower precision is faster, but

1202
00:47:31,600 --> 00:47:36,240
accuracy fails first.

1203
00:47:34,200 --> 00:47:38,120
Navigating between those two facts is a

1204
00:47:36,240 --> 00:47:39,840
discipline of its own called

1205
00:47:38,120 --> 00:47:41,640
quantization.

1206
00:47:39,840 --> 00:47:43,480
That is exactly where this series goes

1207
00:47:41,640 --> 00:47:47,120
next.

1208
00:47:43,480 --> 00:47:49,320
Before that, look at what stayed fixed.

1209
00:47:47,120 --> 00:47:51,840
Four generations of FlashAttention, each

1210
00:47:49,320 --> 00:47:53,040
in its headline precision, compute exact

1211
00:47:51,840 --> 00:47:56,400
attention.

1212
00:47:53,040 --> 00:47:58,960
The same S equals Q times K transpose,

1213
00:47:56,400 --> 00:48:01,440
the same row-wise softmax, the same

1214
00:47:58,960 --> 00:48:04,200
